  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "How does a 4-to-1 multiplexer work?",
      "a": "It has four data inputs, two select lines and one output. The 2-bit select code opens one of four AND gates, and an OR gate passes that input to the output, so select 1 0 sends D2 through.",
      "asks": [
        "What is a mux?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How many select lines does an 8-input mux need?",
      "a": "Three, because 2 to the power 3 is 8. In general a multiplexer with 2 to the n inputs needs n select lines.",
      "asks": [
        "How many select bits does a multiplexer need?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What does a 2-to-4 decoder do?",
      "a": "It turns a 2-bit binary code into one active line out of four. Input 0 0 activates Y0, 0 1 activates Y1, 1 0 activates Y2 and 1 1 activates Y3.",
      "asks": [
        "What is a decoder in digital electronics?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is address decoding?",
      "a": "A processor puts an address on its bus, and decoders turn the top address bits into chip-select signals, so exactly one memory or device chip responds while the others ignore the bus.",
      "asks": [
        "How does a CPU choose which chip to use?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is the difference between a multiplexer and a demultiplexer?",
      "a": "A multiplexer chooses one of many inputs and sends it to one output. A demultiplexer does the reverse, sending one input to one of many outputs, chosen by the select lines.",
      "asks": [
        "Mux vs demux?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "A multiplexer with n select lines chooses among 2ⁿ inputs.",
    "The 74HC138 is a common 3-to-8 line decoder chip.",
